
MCP-MutBench -- Theorem 1 demonstration  (n=100 per cell)
ASR measured from out-of-band effect oracle, not from server response.

Mutation                    Tier                      D-none                    D-hash                    D-resp
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
M1 hidden instruction       T0         ASR   0.0 / DBR   0.0     ASR   0.0 / DBR 100.0     ASR   0.0 / DBR   0.0
M4 behavior-only naive      T1         ASR 100.0 / DBR   0.0     ASR 100.0 / DBR   0.0     ASR   0.0 / DBR 100.0
M5 behavior-only ADAPTIVE   T2         ASR 100.0 / DBR   0.0     ASR 100.0 / DBR   0.0     ASR 100.0 / DBR   0.0

Reading of the result
------------------------------------------------------------
  Response validation vs T1 (M4):  DBR 100.0%  <- looks like a defense
  Response validation vs T2 (M5):  DBR 0.0%  <- Theorem 1
  Hash pinning vs T2 (M5):         DBR 0.0%

  Both M4 and M5 produce the SAME malicious effect. The only
  difference is that M5 also forges the response -- one line of
  attacker code -- and every response-inspecting defense goes to
  zero. Detection here is not degraded; it is information-
  theoretically absent: the transcripts are identical.

  Caveat: M1's ASR is 0 here because this harness has no LLM in
  the loop -- a hidden instruction needs an agent to read and act
  on it. M1's ASR is measured in the full LLM experiments; the
  cell above only shows that hash pinning detects it (DBR 100%).

